Why each element was added
---------------------------

Extra server (4th server total)
  The previous design had all components crammed onto each server. Now each layer
  gets its own dedicated machine: one for Nginx, one for the app, one for the
  database. That's three component servers plus the new one brings us to a proper
  split setup.

Second HAproxy load balancer (clustered with the first)
  The load balancer was a single point of failure in the previous design. By
  clustering two HAproxy instances together, if one goes down the other takes over
  automatically. Traffic never stops flowing. The two LBs share a virtual IP and
  one is active while the other is on standby, ready to step in.

Splitting components onto separate servers
  Putting the web server, app server, and database each on their own machine means:
  - No more resource competition between layers
  - You can scale each layer independently (e.g. add more app servers without
    touching the database server)
  - A crash or overload in one layer doesn't drag the others down
  - Easier to secure and maintain each component separately

Web server vs application server — why they're separate
  The web server (Nginx) only handles HTTP — serving static files, managing
  connections, and routing requests. It doesn't need to run code.
  The application server runs the actual business logic — it executes code,
  processes data, and talks to the database. Mixing them on the same machine
  means a spike in app processing eats into the web server's ability to handle
  connections, and vice versa. Keeping them separate makes both faster and
  easier to scale.


